fn is_palindrome(s: &str) -> bool {
    let bytes = s.as_bytes();
    let mut i = 0usize;
    let mut j = bytes.len();

    while i < j {
        // Skip from the left to the next alphanumeric character.
        while i < j && !bytes[i].is_ascii_alphanumeric() {
            i += 1;
        }
        // Skip from the right to the previous alphanumeric character.
        while i < j && !bytes[j - 1].is_ascii_alphanumeric() {
            j -= 1;
        }
        if i < j {
            let a = bytes[i].to_ascii_lowercase();
            let b = bytes[j - 1].to_ascii_lowercase();
            if a != b {
                return false;
            }
            i += 1;
            j -= 1;
        }
    }
    true
}

fn main() {
    let cases = [
        ("A man, a plan, a canal: Panama", true),
        ("race a car", false),
        ("", true),
        ("!!!", true),
        ("Was it a car or a cat I saw?", true),
    ];
    for (input, expected) in cases {
        assert_eq!(is_palindrome(input), expected, "input: {input:?}");
    }
    println!("all passed");
}
```

Two indices converge from both ends, skipping characters that are not ASCII alphanumeric and comparing lowercase bytes, so nothing is allocated and the slice is touched once. Empty strings and strings with no alphanumerics are correctly true because the pointers meet without a mismatch. If you need full Unicode folds instead of ASCII, this fast path should be swapped for `char`-based iteration, which I can show on request.

